## 4. GameEngine command + `switchDifficulty()`

### `lib/game/engine/commands.ts`

Add to the `Command` union: `| { type: "setDifficulty"; difficulty: Difficulty }`. Import `{ Difficulty } from "@/lib/game/gameModes"` — actually from `@/lib/game/difficulties`.

### `lib/game/engine/GameEngine.ts` — dispatch case + switcher

Dispatch (~line 451, next to `setGameMode`):
```typescript
case "setDifficulty": {
  this.switchDifficulty(command.difficulty);
  break;
}
```

New private method, mirroring `switchGameMode` but lighter (difficulty doesn't touch flight/noclip/panels):
```typescript
/**
 * Switches the live difficulty (from the pause menu). Re-applies immediately:
 * dropping to Peaceful despawns every hostile (and resets the starvation timer);
 * raising difficulty just changes the multipliers the directors/AI read next tick.
 */
private switchDifficulty(next: Difficulty): void {
  const state = this.state;
  if (!isDifficulty(next) || next === state.difficulty) return; // no isDead guard needed
  state.difficulty = next;
  state.timers.starvationTimer = 0;
  if (!hostilesSpawn(next)) this.despawnHostiles();
}

/** Removes every hostile mob (Peaceful switch). Descending so splices don't shift indices. */
private despawnHostiles(): void {
  const mobs = this.state.mobs;
  for (let i = mobs.length - 1; i >= 0; i -= 1) {
    if (mobs[i].hostile) this.removeMobAt(i);
  }
}
```

Design notes:
- **No `isDead` guard** (unlike `switchGameMode`, which bails when dead to avoid mid-respawn state churn). Difficulty has no respawn-coupled invariants, and a player should be able to flip to Peaceful from the death screen to stop a hostile pile-on. (Optionally mirror the `isDead` guard for symmetry — but allowing it while dead is more user-friendly. Recommend: no guard, but reset `starvationTimer` regardless.)
- **Despawn uses the existing `removeMobAt`** (line 614), which is the canonical mob-removal path (handles any cleanup). The boss is `hostile: true` so it would be despawned too — acceptable, since switching to Peaceful mid-boss-fight is an explicit "make it stop" action. Document this.
- Add imports: `{ isDifficulty, hostilesSpawn, type Difficulty } from "@/lib/game/difficulties"`.

### Step-loop wiring (~lines 294–309)

Add the starvation tick right after `tickHungerDrain`/`tickHealthRegen` (so it reads the freshly-drained hunger):
```typescript
tickStarvation(state, dt, this.applyStarvationFloored, this.applyEnvironmentalDamage);
```
Where `applyStarvationFloored` is a new small arrow method next to `applyPoisonDamage`:
```typescript
/** Starvation chip (Easy/Normal): armor-bypassing, floored at the difficulty's HP. */
private applyStarvationFloored = (amount: number, floorHp: number): void => {
  if (applyNonLethalDamage(this.state, amount, floorHp)) this.emit({ type: "playerHurt" });
};
```
And the lethal path reuses the existing `this.applyEnvironmentalDamage` (armor-bypassing, can kill, emits `died`). This is the cleanest mapping: Easy/Normal → floored non-lethal; Hard (floor 0) → environmental lethal.

The spawn-director calls (`tickHostileSpawnDirector`, `tickSpawnerDirector`) and `tickMobs` already run in the loop — they now read `state.difficulty` internally, no call-site change.

