```

New private method, mirroring `switchGameMode` but lighter (difficulty doesn't touch flight/noclip/panels):
```typescript
/**
 * Switches the live difficulty (from the pause menu). Re-applies immediately:
 * dropping to Peaceful despawns every hostile (and resets the starvation timer);
 * raising difficulty just changes the multipliers the directors/AI read next tick.
 */
private switchDifficulty(next: Difficulty): void {
  const state = this.state;
  if (!isDifficulty(next) || next === state.difficulty) return; // no isDead guard needed
  state.difficulty = next;
  state.timers.starvationTimer = 0;
  if (!hostilesSpawn(next)) this.despawnHostiles();
}

/** Removes every hostile mob (Peaceful switch). Descending so splices don't shift indices. */
private despawnHostiles(): void {
  const mobs = this.state.mobs;
  for (let i = mobs.length - 1; i >= 0; i -= 1) {
    if (mobs[i].hostile) this.removeMobAt(i);
  }
}
```

Design notes:
- **No `isDead` guard** (unlike `switchGameMode`, which bails when dead to avoid mid-respawn state churn). Difficulty has no respawn-coupled invariants, and a player should be able to flip to Peaceful from the death screen to stop a hostile pile-on. (Optionally mirror the `isDead` guard for symmetry — but allowing it while dead is more user-friendly. Recommend: no guard, but reset `starvationTimer` regardless.)
- **Despawn uses the existing `removeMobAt`** (line 614), which is the canonical mob-removal path (handles any cleanup). The boss is `hostile: true` so it would be despawned too — acceptable, since switching to Peaceful mid-boss-fight is an explicit "make it stop" action. Document this.
- Add imports: `{ isDifficulty, hostilesSpawn, type Difficulty } from "@/lib/game/difficulties"`.

### Step-loop wiring (~lines 294–309)

Add the starvation tick right after `tickHungerDrain`/`tickHealthRegen` (so it reads the freshly-drained hunger):
```typescript
tickStarvation(state, dt, this.applyStarvationFloored, this.applyEnvironmentalDamage);
```
Where `applyStarvationFloored` is a new small arrow method next to `applyPoisonDamage`:
```typescript
/** Starvation chip (Easy/Normal): armor-bypassing, floored at the difficulty's HP. */
private applyStarvationFloored = (amount: number, floorHp: number): void => {
  if (applyNonLethalDamage(this.state, amount, floorHp)) this.emit({ type: "playerHurt" });
};
```
And the lethal path reuses the existing `this.applyEnvironmentalDamage` (armor-bypassing, can kill, emits `died`). This is the cleanest mapping: Easy/Normal → floored non-lethal; Hard (floor 0) → environmental lethal.

The spawn-director calls (`tickHostileSpawnDirector`, `tickSpawnerDirector`) and `tickMobs` already run in the loop — they now read `state.difficulty` internally, no call-site change.

## 5. UI changes

### `components/menu/CreateWorldForm.tsx`

- Import `{ DIFFICULTY_PRESETS, type Difficulty } from "@/lib/game/difficulties";`.
- `onCreate` prop signature: `(name, seed, worldType, gameMode, difficulty) => void`.
- `const [difficulty, setDifficulty] = useState<Difficulty>("normal");`.
- Add a "Difficulty" `menu-field` with a `world-type-grid` of `DIFFICULTY_PRESETS` buttons (copy the Game Mode block at lines 39–56 verbatim, swapping presets/state). Place it between Game Mode and World Type for a natural difficulty-near-mode grouping.
- Pass `difficulty` in the `onCreate(...)` submit call.

### `components/menu/WorldSelect.tsx`

- `onCreate` callback: accept `difficulty` and pass to `createWorld(profile.id, name, seed, { worldType, gameMode, difficulty })` (~line 39).
- Add a `difficultyLabel(id)` helper (mirror `gameModeLabel`, ~line 15) reading `DIFFICULTY_PRESETS`.
- World card line (~line 106): optionally surface non-normal difficulty: `{world.difficulty !== "normal" ? \`${difficultyLabel(world.difficulty)} · \` : ""}`. Mirror the gameMode-on-card pattern (only show when non-default).
- Import `{ DIFFICULTY_PRESETS, type Difficulty }`.

### `components/game/PauseMenu.tsx`

- Props: add `difficulty: Difficulty;` and `onDifficultyChange: (difficulty: Difficulty) => void;`.
- Import `{ DIFFICULTY_PRESETS, type Difficulty }`.
- In the Game tab, after the existing `pause-modes` Game Mode block (~line 142), add a parallel `pause-modes` block titled "Difficulty" rendering `DIFFICULTY_PRESETS` buttons calling `onDifficultyChange(preset.id)` with `aria-pressed={preset.id === difficulty}`. Reuse `pause-modes-grid` / `mode-option` classes — no new CSS needed.

### `components/MinecraftGame.tsx`

- Destructure `difficulty` and `setDifficulty` from the hook (next to `gameMode`/`setGameMode`, ~lines 35–37).
- Pass `difficulty={difficulty}` and `onDifficultyChange={setDifficulty}` to `<PauseMenu>` (~lines 175–176).
- (No HUD change strictly required; difficulty isn't shown in-HUD in Minecraft. Skip unless desired.)

### `lib/game/useMinecraftGame.ts`

- `PRE_MOUNT_SNAPSHOT`: add `difficulty: "normal",` (~line 37).
- `worldDifficultyRef = useRef(opts.world.difficulty);` mirroring `worldModeRef` (~line 105).
- Engine construction options (~lines 170–171): add `difficulty: worldDifficultyRef.current`.
- Return object (~lines 368–370): add `difficulty: snapshot.difficulty,` and `setDifficulty: (difficulty: Difficulty) => engine?.dispatch({ type: "setDifficulty", difficulty }),`.
- Import `{ type Difficulty } from "@/lib/game/difficulties"`.

## 6. Test plan

### `lib/game/difficulties.test.ts` (NEW) — mirror `gameModes.test.ts`

- `isDifficulty`: accepts the four ids, rejects `["", "Peaceful", "survival", "hardcore", null, undefined, 2, {}]`.
- `presets`: `DIFFICULTY_PRESETS.map(p => p.id)` deep-equals `[...DIFFICULTY_IDS]`.
- **Accessor matrix** — a `Record<Difficulty, {...}>` table (the source of truth per level), asserting `hostilesSpawn`, `mobDamageMultiplier`, `hostileSpawnIntervalScale`, `hostileCapScale`, `regenIntervalScale`, `starvationFloorHp`, `starves` for each id, looping over `DIFFICULTY_IDS`. This is the single intent table reviewers read.

### Starvation unit tests (`playerStats` — extend existing test file or new `playerStats.starvation.test.ts`)

Build a minimal `GameState` (the existing playerStats tests show the pattern) and assert via fake `applyFloored`/`applyLethal` spies + direct `state.hearts` checks:
- Peaceful: hunger 0, advance many intervals → no damage call, hearts unchanged.
- Easy: hunger 0, hearts 20 → chips by STARVATION_HP per interval down to 10, then stops (never below floor).
- Normal: floors at 1 (½ heart), never 0.
- Hard: floor 0 → routes through the lethal callback; can reach 0 (death).
- Gate checks: `state.hunger > 0` → no-op and timer reset; `takesDamage(gameMode) === false` (creative) → no-op; `isDead` → no-op.
- Timer accumulation across small `dt` slices (sub-interval ticks don't fire; crossing the interval fires exactly once; a big `dt` can fire multiple).

### Spawn-gating units (`spawnDirector` — extend/new test file)

- `spawnInitialMobs` on Peaceful: no `hostile: true` mob lands in `state.mobs` (only passives); on Normal: hostiles present.
- `tickHostileSpawnDirector` on Peaceful: early-returns, mob count unchanged even at night/past interval.
- Interval scaling: with a fixed `rng` and forced night, Hard fires sooner than Normal sooner than Easy across controlled `dt` advances (assert timer threshold via `HOSTILE_SPAWN_INTERVAL_SECONDS * scale`).
- Cap scaling: pre-seed `state.mobs` with hostiles to `Math.round(HOSTILE_CAP * scale)` and assert no further spawn; one below the scaled cap → spawns.
- `tickSpawnerDirector` on Peaceful: early-returns even with active spawners + player in range.

### Engine `switchDifficulty` integration (extend GameEngine test suite)

- Construct an engine, spawn hostiles (or use `spawnInitialMobs` on Normal), `dispatch({ type: "setDifficulty", difficulty: "peaceful" })`, assert `state.mobs.every(m => !m.hostile)` and `state.difficulty === "peaceful"`.
- Switch Peaceful→Hard: hostiles do NOT auto-spawn instantly (directors trickle), `state.difficulty === "hard"`.
- `serialize()` round-trips `difficulty` at `version: 9`; `restoreDifficulty` defaults a v8 save to "normal".

### Save migration units (extend `save.test.ts`)

- `migrateSaveV8toV9` is a pure version bump preserving all fields.
- `readSave` chains v7→v8→v9 and a raw v9 with `difficulty: "hard"` restores it; an unknown `difficulty` value falls back via `restoreDifficulty` to "normal".

### Worlds unit (extend `worlds.test.ts`)

- `createWorld(..., { difficulty: "hard" })` persists `difficulty: "hard"`; omitted → "normal"; garbage → "normal" via `sanitizeWorld`.

## 7. Docs / changelog

- **`docs/save-format.md`**: add the v9 row (difficulty, optional, defaults "normal", switchable like gameMode), update the "current version" references.
- **`docs/tuning.md`**: new section "Difficulty" documenting the accessor table (the §1 numeric table) and the new `STARVATION_INTERVAL_SECONDS` / `STARVATION_HP` config constants; note that difficulty multipliers live in `difficulties.ts`, not `config.ts`, and why.
- **`docs/architecture.md`**: add difficulty as an orthogonal axis beside gameMode; describe the accessor-predicate pattern, the despawn-on-Peaceful switch, and the starvation tick's place in the step loop. Update the `gameModes.ts` lines 10–11 deferral comment is now resolved — note in architecture that difficulty was the deferred axis now implemented.
- **`docs/manual.md` + `docs/reference.md`**: player-facing difficulty table (what each level does to mobs, regen, hunger/starvation), and that it's chosen at creation + switchable in the pause menu Game tab.
- **`README.md`**: one line in the feature list ("Four difficulty levels — Peaceful to Hard").
- **`CHANGELOG.md`**: a `feat` entry under the current unreleased section summarizing difficulty levels, scaled mob damage + spawns, peaceful despawn, and the new starvation mechanic; note save bump to v9.
- **Update the `gameModes.ts` deferral comment** (lines 10–11): change "deferred to a later change" to reference that difficulty now lives in `difficulties.ts` (keeps the two-axis story coherent; Hardcore remains deferred).

## 8. Commit slicing (Conventional Commits, focused vertical slices)

Branch: `feat/game-difficulty` (this is multi-file feature work — branch off the default).

1. **`feat(difficulty): add Difficulty enum, guards, presets and accessors`** — `lib/game/difficulties.ts` + `difficulties.test.ts` + the two `config.ts` starvation constants. Pure, fully tested, no wiring. (Tracer-bullet core.)
2. **`feat(save): persist difficulty (save v9, world meta)`** — `types.ts` v9, `save.ts` migration + `restoreDifficulty`, `worlds.ts` WorldMeta/createWorld/sanitize, plus migration/worlds tests. Self-contained persistence slice.
3. **`feat(engine): thread difficulty through state, command and live switch`** — `state.ts` (field + timer + snapshot), `GameEngine.ts` (options, restore, serialize, `setDifficulty` command, `switchDifficulty`/`despawnHostiles`), `commands.ts`, + engine switch test. State plumbing only.
4. **`feat(difficulty): scale mob damage and gate hostile spawns`** — `mobAI.ts` (3 damage sites), `spawnDirector.ts` (initial + director + spawner gating/scaling), + spawn-gating tests. The combat/spawn behavior slice.
5. **`feat(difficulty): difficulty-scaled hunger starvation and faster peaceful regen`** — `playerStats.ts` (`tickStarvation`, regen scaling), `GameEngine.ts` step-loop call + `applyStarvationFloored`, + starvation tests. The new-mechanic slice.
6. **`feat(ui): pick difficulty at creation and switch it in the pause menu`** — `CreateWorldForm.tsx`, `WorldSelect.tsx`, `PauseMenu.tsx`, `MinecraftGame.tsx`, `useMinecraftGame.ts`. The UI slice (depends on 1–3).
7. **`docs(difficulty): document levels, starvation and save v9`** — all docs + CHANGELOG + the gameModes.ts comment update.

Each commit compiles and passes tests on its own (slices 1–5 are behavior-complete behind the default "normal", so even partway the game stays playable). End commit messages with the required `Co-Authored-By` trailer.

## 9. Tricky interactions (call-outs)

- **Difficulty × gameMode independence.** They are fully orthogonal. `mobsThreaten(gameMode)` (creative/spectator → false) already makes hostiles ignore the player; `hostilesSpawn(difficulty)` (peaceful → false) governs whether hostiles *exist*. **Peaceful stops hostile spawns regardless of gameMode** — a creative+Peaceful world has zero hostiles, which is the intuitive result. Conversely creative+Hard spawns thick hordes that simply can't hurt the invulnerable player. The spawn gate keys ONLY on difficulty; the damage/threat gate keys ONLY on gameMode. Don't cross-couple them.
- **Starvation only fires under `takesDamage(gameMode)`.** Creative/spectator never starve (their hunger is pinned to MAX by `tickHungerDrain`'s early return anyway), so the `takesDamage` guard in `tickStarvation` is belt-and-suspenders but correct. Easy/Normal use the floored non-lethal path (reusing `applyNonLethalDamage`, exactly the Poison precedent); only Hard (floor 0) routes through the lethal environmental-damage path that emits `died` and triggers respawn.
- **No prior starvation baseline.** Today hunger hits 0 with zero consequence. This feature adds the *first* consequence. On the default "normal", a hunger-0 player now chips to ½ heart — a real behavior change for existing worlds. Flag in CHANGELOG. The ½-heart Normal floor (not death) keeps it forgiving and matches the "Normal starves down to ~1 HP floor" spec.
- **Live re-application.** Spawn directors and mob AI read `state.difficulty` every tick, so raising/lowering difficulty takes effect on the *next* tick automatically — no re-init. The only thing requiring explicit action on switch is **dropping to Peaceful**, which must actively despawn the hostiles already present (`despawnHostiles()` in `switchDifficulty`) since the directors only control *new* spawns. Resetting `starvationTimer` on every switch prevents a stale accumulator from instantly chipping after a Peaceful→Hard flip.
- **Boss carve-out.** Totem-summoned boss + minions bypass the spawn directors by design. `switchDifficulty(peaceful)` *will* despawn an active boss (it's `hostile`), which is the desired "panic button" behavior; but the directors never gate boss summoning by difficulty. Boss/minion attack damage *does* scale via the mobAI multiplier sites. Document both.
- **Cap rounding.** `Math.round(HOSTILE_CAP * hostileCapScale)` — Easy → 8, Hard → 24, all integers with the chosen scales, so rounding is a no-op here but keeps the cap an integer if scales change later.
- **Mitigated-damage floor.** `applyDamageWithArmor` floors at `Math.max(1, ...)`, so Easy's 0.5× never zeroes a hit. Intended and Minecraft-faithful — Easy softens, never neutralizes, melee.

---

## Critical Files for Implementation
- /Users/hutusi/workspace/ai/david/monecraft/lib/game/difficulties.ts (new — enum, guard, presets, accessors)
- /Users/hutusi/workspace/ai/david/monecraft/lib/game/engine/GameEngine.ts (options, restore/serialize, setDifficulty command, switchDifficulty/despawnHostiles, step-loop starvation call)
- /Users/hutusi/workspace/ai/david/monecraft/lib/game/engine/systems/playerStats.ts (new tickStarvation, regen scaling)
- /Users/hutusi/workspace/ai/david/monecraft/lib/game/engine/systems/spawnDirector.ts (Peaceful gate, scaled interval/cap)
- /Users/hutusi/workspace/ai/david/monecraft/lib/game/save.ts (v9 migration, restoreDifficulty) — paired with /Users/hutusi/workspace/ai/david/monecraft/lib/game/types.ts (v9 shape)

